{
  "protocol": "dhcp",
  "title": "DHCP",
  "source": "RFC 2131 (message format) and RFC 2132 (options)",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "dhcp",
      "kind": "protocol",
      "as": "DHCP",
      "about": [
        "RFC 2131 §2: 'DHCP uses the BOOTP message format', and §2 Figure 1 gives ONE format. So there is one",
        "message here. DHCPDISCOVER and DHCPOFFER are not two formats — they are two sets of values in the same",
        "one, told apart by option 53, which is a value buried in a list at a position the sender chooses.",
        "",
        "That is worth saying plainly because it is the opposite of MQTT, where six formats are told apart by a",
        "nibble at a fixed offset. `identifies` reads a path to a discriminator; DHCP's discriminator is not on",
        "a path, it is an item of a list, so nothing here identifies anything and nothing needs to.",
        "",
        "WHAT IS ACTUALLY HARD is the option region. RFC 2132 §2 gives options three shapes, not one: a bare",
        "PAD (0x00) and a bare END (0xFF) are a single octet with no length and no value, and everything else",
        "is code, length, and that many octets. Point a fixed code-length-value walker at a DISCOVER and it",
        "reads the END's code, takes the PAD octet after it as a LENGTH, and swallows the trailing fill as a",
        "value — after which the remaining zeros decode as a dozen phantom options. Describing the SHAPES",
        "rather than the codes is what makes that impossible, the same way it is for a TCP option.",
        "",
        "HOW MANY OPTIONS THERE ARE is nowhere in the message. Going out the region is written once per item of",
        "the list it requires; coming in the reading goes round until it reads the END. Neither direction has a",
        "count and neither needs one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dhcpMessage",
      "kind": "message",
      "as": "DHCP message",
      "about": [
        "RFC 2131 §2 Figure 1, plus the options region RFC 2132 defines. 236 octets of fixed BOOTP fields, the",
        "four-octet magic cookie that tells DHCP from bare BOOTP, and then options to the END."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "packing.dhcpMessage",
      "kind": "packing",
      "as": "the one arrangement"
    },
    {
      "id": "bootp",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "the fixed-format part",
      "about": [
        "The 236 octets every BOOTP message begins with, whichever DHCP message type it turns out to carry."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "op",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "op",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 1,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": "Which direction this is going. Not which DHCP message it is — a DISCOVER and a REQUEST are both BOOTREQUESTs, and what tells them apart is option 53."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.op",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "op",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: 1 for a BOOTREQUEST, 2 for a BOOTREPLY.",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "htype",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "htype",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 1,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "input.htype",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "htype",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: the hardware address type — 1 for Ethernet.",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "hlen",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "hlen",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 1,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": [
        "How many of chaddr's sixteen octets are the hardware address. Not supplied: it is the length of the",
        "address itself, so the two cannot disagree — which they can on a wire, and a message where they do is",
        "one where the padding is read as part of the address."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "hops",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "hops",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 1,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "input.hops",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "hops",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: relay agents increment this; a client sets 0.",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "xid",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "xid",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 4,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": "The correlation token for a whole acquisition. Every retransmission of one attempt must reuse it, which is a fact about a conversation rather than about this message."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.xid",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "xid",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: the transaction id, chosen by the client and echoed by the server.",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "secs",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "secs",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 2,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "input.secs",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "secs",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: seconds since the client began acquiring, which must grow across retransmissions of one transaction.",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "flags",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "flags",
      "about": [
        "RFC 2131 §2 Figure 2: one broadcast bit and fifteen that must be zero, spanning two octets. A set,",
        "because the RFC draws it as one — and two fields, because the bit that matters is answerable only if",
        "it is a node. It also settles that a run of bits is not confined to an octet: fifteen bits is fifteen",
        "bits, and what occupies whole octets is the set."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "broadcastFlag",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "Broadcast Flag",
      "form": {
        "of": "run",
        "bits": 1
      },
      "about": "RFC 2131 §4.1: set when the client cannot yet receive a unicast reply, because it has no address to receive it at."
    },
    {
      "id": "flagsReserved",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "MBZ",
      "form": {
        "of": "run",
        "bits": 15
      },
      "about": "RFC 2131 §2: \"MUST BE ZERO\"."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.broadcastFlag",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "Broadcast Flag",
      "purpose": "1 to ask the server to broadcast its reply, 0 to accept a unicast one.",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "constant.zeroBits",
      "kind": "constant",
      "label": "zero",
      "holds": {
        "int": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ciaddr",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "ciaddr",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque",
        "octets": 4
      },
      "via": "ipv4",
      "about": "Four octets on the wire and an address to anyone setting it, which is what `via` is for: the conversion goes one way going out and is inverted coming in, so there is one statement of it rather than a caller and a reader that have to agree by hand."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.ciaddr",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "ciaddr",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: the client's current address, and zero until it has one.",
      "gives": "Text"
    },
    {
      "id": "yiaddr",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "yiaddr",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque",
        "octets": 4
      },
      "via": "ipv4",
      "about": "Four octets on the wire and an address to anyone setting it, which is what `via` is for: the conversion goes one way going out and is inverted coming in, so there is one statement of it rather than a caller and a reader that have to agree by hand."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.yiaddr",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "yiaddr",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: the address the server is offering or assigning.",
      "gives": "Text"
    },
    {
      "id": "siaddr",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "siaddr",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque",
        "octets": 4
      },
      "via": "ipv4",
      "about": "Four octets on the wire and an address to anyone setting it, which is what `via` is for: the conversion goes one way going out and is inverted coming in, so there is one statement of it rather than a caller and a reader that have to agree by hand."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.siaddr",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "siaddr",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: the next server to use in bootstrap.",
      "gives": "Text"
    },
    {
      "id": "giaddr",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "giaddr",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque",
        "octets": 4
      },
      "via": "ipv4",
      "about": "Four octets on the wire and an address to anyone setting it, which is what `via` is for: the conversion goes one way going out and is inverted coming in, so there is one statement of it rather than a caller and a reader that have to agree by hand."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.giaddr",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "giaddr",
      "purpose": "RFC 2131 §2: the relay agent that forwarded this, and zero when none did.",
      "gives": "Text"
    },
    {
      "id": "chaddr",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "chaddr",
      "about": [
        "A sixteen-octet slot of which hlen are the hardware address and the rest is fill. So it is two fields:",
        "the address, and what follows it — because the boundary between them is a fact the message states, and",
        "a single opaque sixteen would put the fill inside the address."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "hardwareAddress",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "chaddr",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque"
      },
      "via": "mac",
      "about": "RFC 2131 §2: the client hardware address, hlen octets of it."
    },
    {
      "id": "chaddrPad",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "chaddr fill",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque"
      },
      "about": "The rest of the sixteen, zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.chaddr",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "chaddr",
      "gives": "Text",
      "purpose": "The client hardware address, written the way a hardware address is written. Its length is what hlen says."
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.hlen",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "octets of hardware address",
      "runs": "hardwareAddressExtent",
      "gives": "Int",
      "takes": {
        "hardwareAddressExtent": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.chaddrExtent",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "octets of hardware address, from hlen",
      "runs": "hlen",
      "gives": "Int",
      "takes": {
        "hlen": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.chaddrPad",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "the zeros after the hardware address",
      "runs": "repeat(unhex('00'), 16 - hardwareAddressExtent)",
      "gives": "Bytes",
      "takes": {
        "hardwareAddressExtent": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.chaddrPadExtent",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "octets of fill, from hlen",
      "runs": "16 - hlen",
      "gives": "Int",
      "takes": {
        "hlen": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sname",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "sname",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque",
        "octets": 64
      },
      "about": [
        "RFC 2131 §2: sixty-four octets holding a NUL-terminated server host name, or all zero.",
        "",
        "One field rather than a name and its fill, and that is a limitation rather than a reading of the RFC:",
        "where the name ends is said by the first NUL, and a span that ends at a value it has to find first is",
        "a capability this engine does not have. So what a caller sets is a name, and what the field holds is",
        "the whole sixty-four — `fit` supplies the difference, and does nothing at all when handed sixty-four",
        "octets already, which is what makes reading one back and writing it out again give the same octets."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "file",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "file",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque",
        "octets": 128
      },
      "about": "RFC 2131 §2: a hundred and twenty-eight octets of boot file name, on the same terms as sname."
    },
    {
      "id": "input.sname",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "sname",
      "purpose": "The server host name — text, or the sixty-four octets of the field itself.",
      "gives": "Bytes"
    },
    {
      "id": "input.file",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "file",
      "purpose": "The boot file name — text, or the hundred and twenty-eight octets of the field itself.",
      "gives": "Bytes"
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.sname",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "the server host name, in its sixty-four octets",
      "runs": "fit(sname, 64)",
      "gives": "Bytes",
      "takes": {
        "sname": "Bytes"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.file",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "the boot file name, in its hundred and twenty-eight",
      "runs": "fit(file, 128)",
      "gives": "Bytes",
      "takes": {
        "file": "Bytes"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "magicCookie",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "magic cookie",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 4,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": [
        "RFC 2132 §2: the four octets 99.130.83.99, and the only thing distinguishing a DHCP message from a",
        "bare BOOTP one. Four octets with exactly one legal value, so it is written from a constant and read",
        "against a validator — an unchecked skip past it accepts anything and calls the result DHCP."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "constant.magicCookie",
      "kind": "constant",
      "label": "99.130.83.99",
      "holds": {
        "int": 1669485411
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "validator.magicCookie",
      "kind": "validator",
      "because": "RFC 2132 §2: the magic cookie is 99.130.83.99 and nothing else is DHCP.",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "from": {
            "int": 1669485411
          },
          "to": {
            "int": 1669485411
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "options",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "Options",
      "about": [
        "RFC 2132 §2. How many there are is nowhere in the message, and cannot be worked out in advance either:",
        "an option's width is only known by reading it. So going out this is written once per item of the list",
        "it requires, and coming in the reading goes round until it reads the END option."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "option",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "an option",
      "about": [
        "RFC 2132 §2 gives three shapes, and which applies is the code: 0 is PAD and 255 is END, each a single",
        "octet entire; everything else is code, length, and that many octets. Describing the shapes rather than",
        "the codes means an option nobody here has heard of is read and written correctly — and it is why the",
        "trailing fill of a DISCOVER cannot be mistaken for a run of zero-length options."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "optionCode",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "option code",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 1,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "optionShape",
      "kind": "junction",
      "as": "which shape of option"
    },
    {
      "id": "optionPad",
      "kind": "junction",
      "as": "a pad octet",
      "about": "Code 0. A junction because the code octet is the whole option and there is nothing else to it."
    },
    {
      "id": "optionCounted",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "an option with a length"
    },
    {
      "id": "optionLength",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "option length",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 1,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": "RFC 2132 §2: the octets of value that follow, counting neither itself nor the code."
    },
    {
      "id": "optionValue",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "option value",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque"
      },
      "about": [
        "Whatever the option carries, kept as octets. Reading a lease time here as a number would be this file",
        "deciding that code 51 means something — which is true, and is a different description from this one.",
        "What THIS says is how any option is shaped."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "optionStop",
      "kind": "junction",
      "as": "the end option",
      "about": "Code 255. RFC 2132 §2: the option region ends here, whatever octets follow."
    },
    {
      "id": "optionDone",
      "kind": "junction",
      "as": "the end of this option",
      "about": [
        "Where the three shapes meet. A fork has no single last member, so without somewhere to converge there",
        "is nowhere to ask whether another option is due."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.optionShape",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "which shape of option is this?",
      "runs": "optionCode == 0 ? 0 : (optionCode == 255 ? 2 : 1)",
      "gives": "Int",
      "takes": {
        "optionCode": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.isCounted",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "does this option carry a length?",
      "runs": "optionCode != 0 && optionCode != 255",
      "gives": "Bool",
      "takes": {
        "optionCode": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "input.options",
      "kind": "input",
      "as": "Options",
      "purpose": "The options to write, in order, ending with the END option. Each item says its `code` and, where its shape has one, its `value`.",
      "gives": "List",
      "of": {
        "code": "Int",
        "value": "Bytes"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.eachCode",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "this option's code",
      "runs": "item.code",
      "gives": "Int"
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.eachValue",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "this option's value",
      "runs": "item.value",
      "gives": "Bytes"
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.optionLength",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "octets of value",
      "runs": "optionValueExtent",
      "gives": "Int",
      "takes": {
        "optionValueExtent": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.optionValueExtent",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "octets of value, from the length",
      "runs": "optionLength",
      "gives": "Int",
      "takes": {
        "optionLength": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "trailer",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "trailing fill",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque"
      },
      "about": [
        "RFC 1542 §2.1: a BOOTP message is at least 300 octets, so a short one is padded out. The captures",
        "differ on exactly this — a 267-octet DISCOVER carries 33 octets of fill and a 306-octet OFFER carries",
        "none — which is why the option region cannot be a fixed block: it ends at a sentinel and what follows",
        "is not options.",
        "",
        "The two directions ask different questions here, and that is not an inconsistency. Going out, whether",
        "there is fill is a fact about the minimum size; coming in it is whether there are octets left. Nothing",
        "on the wire says which of the two produced it.",
        "",
        "One span rather than the run of PAD options it nominally is. Recovering it exactly depends on the",
        "writer having padded to the same target — a client padding to 342 decodes identically and writes back",
        "differently, so this round-trips by a shared convention rather than by construction."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.trailer",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "zeros to the three-hundred-octet minimum",
      "runs": "repeat(unhex('00'), 300 - bootpExtent - magicCookieExtent - optionsExtent)",
      "gives": "Bytes",
      "takes": {
        "magicCookieExtent": "Int",
        "optionsExtent": "Int",
        "bootpExtent": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.hasTrailer",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "is any fill needed?",
      "runs": "300 - bootpExtent - magicCookieExtent - optionsExtent > 0",
      "gives": "Bool",
      "takes": {
        "magicCookieExtent": "Int",
        "optionsExtent": "Int",
        "bootpExtent": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.trailerRead",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "is there anything after the end option?",
      "runs": "remaining > 0",
      "gives": "Bool"
    },
    {
      "id": "done",
      "kind": "end-parse",
      "as": "the end of a DHCP message",
      "about": "Where a reading may stop. The option region goes round, and a loop that has run out is indistinguishable from one that was cut short unless something says which endings were legal."
    }
  ],
